{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"tasteful-unschooled-tarpan","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Context / Problem\n\nChanging a ticket's type silently carries forward a plan-review attestation earned under different criteria. Re-run `review-plan` after the change and it reports `runner: reused`, `source: attested`, `model: None` — no LLM runs, and the stored verdict is replayed as if it still applied.\n\nType is not cosmetic to the review: it gates scoring in four places.\n\n- `src/rebar/llm/plan_review/orchestrator.py:364` — `bug_tier = ctx.ticket_type == \"bug\"`\n- `src/rebar/llm/plan_review/orchestrator.py:642` — exempts `bug` / `session_log` / `code_review` / `identity`\n- `src/rebar/llm/plan_review/workflow_ops.py:167` — `session_log` / `code_review` / `identity` are gate-exempt\n- `src/rebar/llm/plan_review/workflow_ops.py:183` — a distinct `bug` branch\n\nSo a plan that PASSES as a `bug` — which is exempt from several criteria — keeps that PASS when retyped to `task`, which is not exempt. The attestation certifies a verdict that was never computed under the criteria now in force.\n\nObserved directly on ticket fec5-d8bb-86cd-453e: created as a `bug`, reviewed PASS, retyped to `task`, re-reviewed → replayed the stored PASS. A forced re-review of the same plan then returned **BLOCK with 3 findings**. The cached verdict was not merely stale, it was wrong for the ticket's current type.\n\n## Root cause\n\n`material_fingerprint` (`src/rebar/llm/plan_review/pass1.py:715`) hashes a basis of `ticket_id`, `description`, `file_impact`, `children`, and conditionally `file_impact_scope` (`:719-730`). `ticket_type` is absent.\n\nThe value is available at the call site — `current_material_fingerprint` builds a `PlanContext` with `ticket_type=state.get(\"ticket_type\", \"\")` (`src/rebar/llm/plan_review/attest.py:778`) — it just never reaches the hash. So a retype leaves the fingerprint identical, the staleness check finds material unchanged, and reuse fires.\n\n## Approach\n\nAdd `ticket_type` to the fingerprint basis in `material_fingerprint`, so a retype changes the hash and invalidates the attestation exactly as a description edit already does.\n\nThis is deliberately the blunt fix, and it has a consequence worth stating plainly: because the basis feeds a `sha256` over the whole dict, adding a key changes the fingerprint for EVERY ticket, not just retyped ones. Every currently-certified attestation becomes stale on its next staleness check, and those tickets need one fresh `review-plan` before their next claim or close. That is a one-time re-review cost across open/in-progress tickets; closed tickets are unaffected in practice since they are not re-gated.\n\nThat cost is accepted rather than engineered around, because it fails in the safe direction: every subsequent attestation is computed under the criteria actually in force, and the alternative preserves the hole for exactly the tickets that already have attestations — the ones most likely to be claimed next.\n\nRejected: store `ticket_type` in the attestation and compare it separately from the fingerprint, leaving existing attestations valid. It sounds more surgical, but the attestation does not currently record `ticket_type` at all (`attest.py` references it only at `:778`, to build the context), so every pre-existing attestation would lack the field. The comparison would then have to fail OPEN for them — preserving the exact defect for every already-certified ticket — or fail CLOSED, which invalidates them anyway and lands in the same place as the simple fix with more moving parts.\n\nRejected: invalidate on retype at the `edit` command instead, by clearing the attestation when `--ticket_type` changes. That fixes the CLI path only; the library `rebar.edit` and the MCP `edit_ticket` tool would still leave a stale attestation, and the staleness check is the single chokepoint every caller already flows through.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/llm/plan_review/pass1.py` — add `ticket_type` to the `basis` dict in `material_fingerprint` (`:719`). Its docstring (`:716-718`) enumerates what counts as material and must be updated to say type is material too.\n- `tests/unit/test_plan_review_attest.py` — cover the fingerprint's sensitivity to type. If that module does not exist, create it.\n\n## Testing\n\n- Failure (the defect): two `PlanContext`s identical except for `ticket_type` produce DIFFERENT fingerprints. This assertion fails against the current implementation, which is what makes it the regression oracle.\n- Happy path: two contexts identical in every material field including type produce the SAME fingerprint, so the change does not make the hash unstable for unchanged tickets.\n- Regression: a description edit still changes the fingerprint, and a non-material change (tags/comments/links/assignee, per the docstring's exclusion list) still does not — proving type was ADDED to the material set rather than the exclusion boundary being redrawn.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] Two `PlanContext` values differing only in `ticket_type` produce different `material_fingerprint` values; verified by the new case in `tests/unit/test_plan_review_attest.py`, which fails against the pre-change implementation.\n- [ ] Two `PlanContext` values identical in all material fields produce the same fingerprint; verified by the stability case in the same module.\n- [ ] A description change still changes the fingerprint and a tags-only change still does not; verified by the two regression cases in the same module.\n- [ ] Retyping a ticket makes a subsequent `review-plan` actually run rather than replay: after `rebar edit <id> --ticket_type=task` on a ticket certified as a `bug`, `rebar review-plan <id>` reports `runner` other than `reused` and emits a sidecar; verified by running it on a scratch ticket and recording the `runner`/`sidecar_emitted` values on this ticket.\n- [ ] `make lint`, `make typecheck` and `pytest tests/unit/test_plan_review_attest.py tests/unit/test_plan_review_reuse.py` all pass; verified by running each.","id":"6e4f-cad1-aa39-446a","parent_id":"","priority":1,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"task","title":"Changing ticket_type must invalidate the plan-review attestation"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1785444730150939001,"uuid":"20e5c98d-38e8-485f-b832-685deb067c59"}